{"records":[{"id":"7y2bm72bgm","title":"Carvacrol alleviates endoplasmic reticulum stress and inflammation induced by lipopolysaccharide by enhancing endoplasmic reticulum autophagy in dairy mammary epithelial cells.","description":"Carvacrol (CAV) exhibits the capacity to augment endoplasmic reticulum autophagy (ER-phagy) in bovine mammary epithelial cells, mitigating endoplasmic reticulum stress (ER-stress) and inflammatory responses, thereby contributing positively to the amelioration of bovine mastitis. By Yang et al. In this paper, the effect of natural plant monomer CAV on enhancing ER-phagy and alleviating ER-stress and inflammation in mammary epithelial cells of dairy cows was discussed in the treatment of mastitis. Through in vivo and in vitro experiments, we found that ER-stress in mastitis tissue was significantly up-regulated, and the ER-phagy pathway, a key pathway that regulates cell homeostasis, was impaired. And CAV was proved to enhance endoplasmic reticulum autophagy. We found that there is a correlation between ER-phagy and ER-stress in dairy mammary epithelial cells, which may be an important direction for the treatment of dairy mastitis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-31T04:50:03.438Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7y2bm72bgm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7y2bm72bgm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"yue","last_name":"yang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Dairy Cattle"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dxvpvnrg7v","title":"The ARLA1A-NAC089 module coordinates ER-phagy with the unfolded protein response (UPR) to maintain cellular homeostasis in plant","description":"The endoplasmic reticulum (ER) is a critical quality-control organelle for protein homeostasis within the cell. The accumulation of misfolded or unfolded proteins triggers ER stress, which can be alleviated through the unfolded protein response (UPR) and ER-phagy. These processes work in concert to preserve ER homeostasis, yet the molecular interactions between them remain poorly understood in plant. In this study, we identify the ER-anchored transcription factor NAC089 as an ER-phagy receptor acting downstream of the ADP-ribosylation factor (ARF)-like (ARL) GTPase ARLA1A under carbon starvation. Furthermore, we demonstrate that active ARLA1A inhibits ER-phagy by negatively regulating NAC089. Notably, the ARLA1A-NAC089 axis coordinates ER-phagy with the UPR to balance cell survival and death. Our findings unveil a multi-layered regulatory network that is essential for maintaining cellular homeostasis and enhancing plant adaptation to environmental stresses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-03T15:16:08.585Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dxvpvnrg7v","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dxvpvnrg7v"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ruixi","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Confocal Microscopy","Western Blot","Image Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"h8p4yh5536","title":"Tubular ER structures shaped by ER-phagy receptors engage in stress-induced Golgi bypass (Song et al)","description":"Original datasets and Supplemental Videos : Tubular ER structures shaped by ER-phagy receptors engage in stress-induced Golgi bypass","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-17T19:20:33.4Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/h8p4yh5536","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/h8p4yh5536"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Min Goo","last_name":"LEE","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Membrane Protein Trafficking","Subcellular Structure","Endoplasmic Reticulum Stress"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dxv5bfjz8s","title":"Data for: The PERK/Nrf2 pathway mediates endoplasmic reticulum stress-induced injury by upregulating endoplasmic reticulophagy in H9c2 cardiomyoblasts","description":"ERS caused ER-phagy with a decrease in cell viability and an increase in cell death in H9c2 cardiomyoblasts.(A) Live/Dead staining of H9c2 cardiomyoblasts was performed using a LIVE/DEAD® Viability/Cytotoxicity Kit (bar=40 μm, n=3). TG- or TM-treated H9c2 cardiomyoblasts were incubated with calcein-AM (green) and EthD-1 (red) for 10 min, and the fluorescence was visualized using a confocal microscope. Green fluorescence indicates live cells, while red fluorescence indicates dead cells. (E) Ultrastructural lesions in the ER caused by TG or TM and the occurrence of ER-phagy were observed via transmission electron microscopy (bar=500 nm). (F) Representative confocal microscopy images corresponding to analysis of ER-phagy for TG- or TM-treated H9c2 cardiomyoblasts and showing colocalization of autophagosomes with ER-fragments (bar=15 μm; n=3). LC3B was labeled with Texas Red, while calreticulin was labeled with Alexa Fluor 488. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-10-17T15:29:53.981Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dxv5bfjz8s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dxv5bfjz8s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xiuhua","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Autophagy","Endoplasmic Reticulum Stress"],"journal_issn":["00243205"]},{"id":"gg7cnt2fyd","title":"sestrin2 drives ER-phagy in response to protein misfolding","description":"Uncropped western blots data for Sestrin2 drives ER-phagy in response to protein misfolding","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-19T08:50:57.524Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gg7cnt2fyd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gg7cnt2fyd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Carmine","last_name":"Settembre","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chiara","last_name":"De leonibus","orcid":null},{"first_name":"marianna","last_name":"maddaluno","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Acquisition"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rttwh5pj7c","title":"ER-phagy and proteostasis defects prime pancreatic epithelial state changes in KRAS-mediated oncogenesis. Salomo Coll et al.","description":"Raw immunoblot data for the study entitled \"ER-phagy and proteostasis defects prime pancreatic epithelial state changes in KRAS-mediated oncogenesis\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-21T14:45:40.383Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rttwh5pj7c","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rttwh5pj7c"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Simon","last_name":"Wilkinson","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/054225q67","name":"Cancer Research UK","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Cancer","Inflammation","Pancreas","Autophagy","Animal Disease Model"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"crzmm5ggzg","title":"Tunicamycin induced ER stress in liver of C57Bl/6N mice","description":"Once protein synthesis is excessive or misfolded protein become aggregated that eventually overwhelm the capacity of the endoplasmic reticulum (ER), a state named ER stress would be reached. ER stress could affect many tissues, especially liver, in which non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, liver steatosis, etc. have been reported relative. But there still lack systematic insight into ER stress in liver, which can be obtained by integrating metabolomics and transcriptomics of the tissue. Here, tunicamycin was utilized to induce ER stress in C57BL/6N mice. Microarray and untargeted metabolomics were performed to identify the genes and metabolites significantly altered. Surprisingly, apart from classical unfolded protein response, liver lipid, arginine and proline metabolism were affirmed to be related with ER stress. And ketone body metabolism changed most prominently in ER stress, with few studies backing. What’s more, succinate receptor 1(Sucnr1) may be a novel marker and therapeutical target of liver ER stress. In this study, combination of metabolome and transcriptome provided reliable information about liver pathological processes, markers and even potential targets involved in liver ER stress.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-04-23T13:43:38.794Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/crzmm5ggzg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/crzmm5ggzg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"M","last_name":"P","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dy97pwyf74","title":"The hyaluronidase, TMEM2, promotes ER homeostasis and longevity independent of the UPRER","description":"Cells have evolved complex mechanisms to maintain protein homeostasis, such as the UPRER, that are strongly associated with several diseases and the aging process. We performed a whole genome CRISPR-based knockout screen to identify genes important for cells to survive ER-based protein misfolding stress. We identified the cell-surface hyaluronidase, Transmembrane Protein 2 (TMEM2), as a potent modulator of ER-stress resistance. The breakdown of the glycosaminoglycan, hyaluronan (HA), by TMEM2 within the extracellular matrix (ECM) altered ER stress resistance independent of canonical UPRER pathways, but dependent upon the cell surface receptor, CD44, a putative HA receptor, and the MAPK cell-signaling components, ERK and p38. Lastly, and most surprisingly, ectopic expression of human TMEM2 in C. elegans protected animals from ER stress and increased both longevity and pathogen resistance independent of canonical UPRER activation, but dependent on the ERK ortholog, mpk-1, and the p38 ortholog, pmk-1. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-09-24T15:51:23.538Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dy97pwyf74","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dy97pwyf74"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ryo","last_name":"Higuchi-Sanabria","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Robert","last_name":"Schinzel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ophir","last_name":"Shalem","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Transcriptome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sy8gyx2bmx","title":"Targeted lipidomics and transcriptomics data of white adipose tissue under ER stress","description":"Endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress is known to impair the function of visceral adipose tissue (VAT) and subcutaneous adipose tissue (SAT), disrupting lipid metabolism. Despite the crucial role lipid plays in regulating adipose tissue function, the specific lipidomic alterations in VAT and SAT under ER stress remain unclear. In this study, ER stress was induced in VAT and SAT, and targeted lipidomic and transcriptomic approaches were applied to assess the profiles of lipid metabolism and gene expression under ER stress condition. . The results revealed that VAT displayed a more pronounced ER stress response, including a marked increase in binding immunoglobulin protein (BiP) expression, and significant lipidomic disruptions, particularly in glycerides and sterols. These disruptions involved a reduction in protective polyunsaturated fatty acyl species and the accumulation of lipotoxic molecules. While SAT exhibited less severe disruptions in lipid metabolism. Transcriptomic analysis further revealed tissue-specific gene expression patterns, with VAT being more susceptible to immune activation, inflammation and metabolic dysfunction, while SAT exhibited alterations predominantly in protein folding processes. These findings provide critical insights into tissue-specific mechanisms of ER stress adaptation in adipose tissues, positioning VAT as a potential therapeutic target for addressing metabolic dysfunction in obesity-related disorders.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-11-11T23:36:11.986Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sy8gyx2bmx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sy8gyx2bmx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ping","last_name":"He","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hvyrpf7cjy","title":"EMBO Reports_ADA and mitophagy","description":"Source data for the manuscript showing Peri-mitochondrial actin filaments regulate Parkin mediated mitophagy via modulation of ER-Mitochondrial contacts.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-11T16:12:01.551Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hvyrpf7cjy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hvyrpf7cjy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rajarshi","last_name":"Chakrabarti","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Actin","Mitochondrion","Mitophagy"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":142004,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":287,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":142004,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":141219,"restricted":72,"embargoed":713},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2312},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1448},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":701},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":473},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":455},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":439},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":423},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":394},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":316},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":275}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":139342},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1908},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":186},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":141},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":80},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":58},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":44},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":982},{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":935},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":529},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":408},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":361}]}}